Solar prominences are cool, dense plasma structures suspended in the hot solar corona by magnetic fields. High-resolution observations reveal dark "bubbles" rising from below the prominence body and developing into upward "plumes" that propagate through the prominence material. Three physical scenarios have been proposed to account for these features:

(i) the bubbles are filled with hot coronal plasma that accumulates in a cavity beneath the prominence and subsequently undergoes condensation, the bubble–prominence interface being subject to a buoyancy-driven (e.g. magnetic Rayleigh–Taylor) instability;

(ii) the rising structures are produced by a thermal instability operating within the prominence–corona system;

(iii) the bubbles correspond to magnetic separatrix surfaces surrounding regions of newly emerging magnetic flux beneath the prominence, in which case the apparent dark rising features are not filled with hot plasma but are simply line-of-sight "windows" through the prominence sheet that reveal the background corona.

Determine which of these three mechanisms — (i) hot plasma filling a cavity prior to condensation, (ii) thermal instability, or (iii) a separatrix around emerging magnetic flux producing optically thin windows — is responsible for the observed bubbles and rising plumes in quiescent solar prominences. In particular, decide whether the plasma inside an observed bubble is hot and dense enough to be physically distinct from the background corona, or whether the bubble interior is consistent with simply being unobscured background corona.

